#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb98d0f-6281-5e2e-9e96-bebf6a6a2157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{1}(r^{2} - 3\\sin θ)rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174073} {:answer/id #uuid "0a06e633-9e34-5e67-95b9-511e37f233c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{1}(1 - 3r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174074} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8581b8-9d27-5325-acba-4e33881e64a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}(1 - 3r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174075} {:answer/id #uuid "48a2dbf8-96f8-5064-a786-4417d12d6445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{1}(r^{2} - 3\\sin θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174076} {:answer/id #uuid "643ef2b4-0a05-5377-8313-4f8fd50c789f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}(r - \\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a06e633-9e34-5e67-95b9-511e37f233c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{1}(1 - 3r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174074} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d2d661-20c1-5077-b682-4d23a2416d62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20585d4c-063f-50c6-8591-a785c62137ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-125719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daab7196-75ee-5175-81bb-5018c5a64555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369226} {:answer/id #uuid "d6115739-19d5-52c3-84c7-d2e689185b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369227} {:answer/id #uuid "9980c91b-cb47-5f99-9c24-10cd17ba48e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369228} {:answer/id #uuid "229a0908-ea5e-5643-9b41-5b5742e54e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369229} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd08c1d-7fcb-5467-8ecf-dc4116090e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd08c1d-7fcb-5467-8ecf-dc4116090e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369230} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a04b29-f889-5a24-a38e-1159cf6cd53d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b97bdd87-b8e7-5fa0-84d3-dcb91e945069" :question/math-academy-id "q-126686" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/e44543ad51864a7db6b8e80b5c560a1ac773a7537573014f737451af4d1209a7.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/d8ee02b2b7282349604dc6305c0b2be796cfdbd2937e06d089e660683a5785ad.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(2xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b13f0e98-1625-5fc5-9463-5ed239808695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369233} {:answer/id #uuid "11b922f4-d37a-5953-933c-664e05647d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369234} {:answer/id #uuid "c18ce837-d0d2-5ba2-8bdf-67e133e2fb1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369235} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6ac347-f7e3-5f27-925b-11f80acce604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369236} {:answer/id #uuid "a6036a58-5415-507a-b060-5e0fcdbaf879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}r(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13f0e98-1625-5fc5-9463-5ed239808695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369233} :answer-field/id #uuid "90afa4b1-b9f6-514b-a164-257986be804d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "208a1083-56ad-5853-aae0-e9bfc216a1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126687" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/e49bde14ba6097dfc7b153d5b93c2be2462b141a37357bfff826c852fc1136eb.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(2-xy+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+xy)\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/8b3c794f11c0a9be558e66c1c7a4ae4cd996037bbd6e491eefaa1a2e9000c596.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=2-xy+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x+4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2-xy+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y-(-x+4y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x-3y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(3x-3y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{8}3{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f8af69-44dc-5e3f-a4ae-eb66ec35dc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369240} {:answer/id #uuid "92045d9d-3bfd-5000-a8a4-1ebec6775423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369241} {:answer/id #uuid "80522476-fae7-5579-8543-aeb55dcd8c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369242} {:answer/id #uuid "645ddde7-ebed-59a9-9543-6eac390d38aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369243} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82bb64-7e72-5ce3-bd7e-f0f588aef175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb82bb64-7e72-5ce3-bd7e-f0f588aef175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369244} :answer-field/id #uuid "093ca155-1c15-5460-a43a-aabbb97c8b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44f0dbe7-0e3a-5de7-ab4d-8d28f09f8e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-126756" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/d675e8d06f2037531a2eec3a7e48374bb0d2e5cb59adfee0cbee5ce3a23add67.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/96998e4a17d20459622486970518a677309b23fd936c993d8c97dcf0d65a9a29.png)\n\n \n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}-(-2{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{r}^{3}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{81}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9879f38c-33ca-5de8-8de2-56c97bbc7e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{90\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369247} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94dc02-10ca-5e75-a954-d2579ea25d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369248} {:answer/id #uuid "2207c320-7528-51ca-badd-e1635a1c99fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369249} {:answer/id #uuid "100f768c-bec2-56ed-8832-0cb281284a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369250} {:answer/id #uuid "00d2476b-0d02-5aca-ba27-7774ad2faa1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2207c320-7528-51ca-badd-e1635a1c99fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369249} :answer-field/id #uuid "3835ccf8-f8a6-5e2c-bc0d-83549731713d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "885d7e93-ddfe-5056-8a1a-3e87c6540035" :question/math-academy-id "q-126758" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/bcdc0f59fa284c2b1f1621e7de057ef6f0e084d2eca6732fbc5eae3f74ede113.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070891/assets/6273034b38059d3d49b2a29c91052c290151b4a29073f1c925172dd1c4c2bf72.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{-{y}^{2}}+3x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{5+x}-3{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{-{y}^{2}}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{2}-(-3{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}60\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =180[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =180\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =90\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
